# What must never enter the backend build context.
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The Dockerfile does `COPY . .` with the context set to `./backend`. Without
# this file that copied 358 MB, of which 347 MB is developer machine state:
# a host virtualenv and three tool caches. Only 11 MB is application code.
#
# The size was the smaller problem. The real one was REPRODUCIBILITY: CI
# builds from a clean checkout and has no `.venv` or caches, so CI and a
# developer's laptop produced DIFFERENT images from the same commit. The
# artefact that passed CI was not the artefact anyone ran locally, and either
# could be the one that shipped.
#
# Everything below is either regenerated by the build, machine-specific, or a
# runtime directory the container creates for itself. Nothing here is
# application source, configuration, a migration or a runtime asset.

# ── The virtualenv: 247 MB, and it is built for the HOST ─────────────────
# Copying a Windows/macOS venv into a linux/amd64 image ships binaries that
# cannot run there. The image installs its own dependencies from
# pyproject.toml in an earlier layer, so this is pure weight — and worse, an
# `/app/.venv` present in the image could shadow the interpreter's own
# site-packages for anything that inspects the working directory.
.venv/
venv/
ENV/
env/

# ── Tool caches: 97 MB combined, all regenerated on demand ───────────────
#
# `**/` ON EVERY NESTED PATTERN, and this is not decoration. `.dockerignore`
# uses Go's filepath.Match, NOT gitignore syntax: a bare `__pycache__/`
# matches only a directory of that name at the CONTEXT ROOT. Written that way
# first, this file still let 536 host `.pyc` files into the image — caught by
# diffing a local build against a clean-checkout build, which is the only
# check that would have found it.
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
**/.mypy_cache
**/.pytest_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/*$py.class

# `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1` is set in the image, so no .pyc is created
# there; these are only ever the host's.

# ── Coverage and test artefacts ──────────────────────────────────────────
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
.tox/
.nox/
junit*.xml

# ── Packaging metadata, regenerated by `pip install -e .` ────────────────
# Stale metadata copied from the host can disagree with the version pip
# resolves inside the image.
*.egg-info/
**/*.egg-info
**/*.egg
build/
dist/
.eggs/

# ── Runtime directories the container creates itself ─────────────────────
# The Dockerfile runs `mkdir -p /app/media /app/staticfiles` and chowns them
# to the runtime user. Copying a host `media/` would bake somebody's local
# uploads into every image and then have them shadowed by the volume anyway.
media/
staticfiles/
static_root/

# ── Secrets: defensive, not descriptive ──────────────────────────────────
# There is no `backend/.env` today — the compose files read the repo-root
# `.env` via `env_file:`, which is never part of this context. These patterns
# exist so that CREATING one later cannot silently bake production
# credentials into a published image layer, where they survive every later
# `RUN rm` because layers are additive.
#
# `.env*.example` is deliberately NOT re-included: templates are useful in the
# repository and pointless in a running container.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
secrets/
credentials.json
service-account*.json

# ── Version control ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `.git` lives at the repository root, one level above this context, so it is
# already excluded by construction. Listed anyway: a future change to the
# build context (a root-level Dockerfile, a monorepo tool) would otherwise
# start copying the entire history into the image without anybody noticing.
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# ── Editor and OS noise ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
.vscode/
.idea/
**/*.swp
**/*.swo
**/*~
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db

# ── Docker's own files ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not needed inside the image, and excluding the Dockerfile keeps a trivial
# comment edit from invalidating the `COPY . .` layer cache.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml

# ── Local scratch ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
**/*.log
**/*.sqlite3
**/*.db
.pre-commit-config.yaml

# NOT excluded, deliberately:
#   apps/**            application code AND all 70 migration files
#   config/**          settings, urls, wsgi, asgi, di
#   core/**            ports, adapters, preflight, health, scheduling
#   docker/**          gunicorn.conf.py — the CMD references it by path
#   manage.py          migrate_safe, run_scheduled_jobs, ensure_admin
#   pyproject.toml     read by pip AND by the django-stubs/mypy config
#   **/tests/**        inert in the image (pytest is a dev extra and is not
#                      installed), and kept so the image contains exactly what
#                      CI validated. Excluding them is a safe future
#                      optimisation — verified that no non-test module imports
#                      anything under a tests package — but it is a change to
#                      image CONTENTS, which is not what this file is fixing.
